OSY <osy@softhome.net> wrote:
> For booting it is true.  In most cases you can install incompatable card and
> use them in the os, but that does not help with booting.

I am willing to bet that I booted and installed many more Alpha
types, not mentioning individual machines, than you did.  A company
I work for sold quite a few of these over years.  There is more
variants which I never touched but I still had a quite reasonable
sample.

If you are booting from a device hooked up to some particular controller
you need that controller on a list of your specific firmware  but, at
least for PCI based boards as opposed to Turbochannel ones (which are
not supported by Linux at all) these controllers are just standard ones.
Indeed NCR and Qlogic were widely used although, IIRC, 164LX will
boot from Adaptec as well provided its SRM was sufficiently updated.
In earlier times with Adaptec you needed ARC, or derivatives, instead
of SRM.

The bigger trouble is actually with video cards.  You need one which
will be initialized by an x86 BIOS emulator provided in your firmware.
Finding such good enough  when older models were vanishing from the
market was a constant headache but those who work are not "special".
An Alpha which still sits on my desk is equipped with a regular Radeon
7500.

  Michal



