Roger wrote:

> Hi,
> Recently I got a DEC3000/600. I'd like to run linux on it, but much to my
> disappointment, the DEC3000/600 comes with the TurboChannel architecture
> (TC) which isn't supported by linux.
> Now I stumbled upon netbsd, and it seems that netbsd has TC support.
> Is it possible (technically and legally) to port the netbsd code to linux
> ?
> roger

I cannot comment on if it's technically possible - this is up to your 
programming abilities but here's my view on legal status (bearing in mind 
I'm not a Lawyer).

The legal side depends on what you intend to do with the ported code - if 
you're porting from one open-source OS to another then there will be no 
problems if the port is only kept to yourself and not distributed (the GPL 
and LGPL licences state that when you distribute code under this licence 
you have to give others the same rights to the code that you have to it - 
it doesn't stop you keeping it undistributed). 

If you intend to distribute the port then you will need to make sure the 
NetBSD licence allows you to redistribute their code under the linux GPL 
licence if you merge it into GPL code - if your ported code is however kept 
separate and only statically linked to LGPL libraries (so it calls LGPL 
licenced code but doesn't contain any GPL or LGPL licenced code) then it 
may still be distributed under the original BSD licence so no problems 
there either.

So in short - if you don't mix BSD code with LGPL or GPL linux code 
(statically linking to LGPL libraries is fine but not GPL libraries) then 
distribution is fine, if you do mix the code but don't distribute the 
result then this is also fine - just be careful if you mix code from 
different sources and want to distribute the result.




