Because I do not know them, I do not have a comprehensive list, and
they differ between various boards.  Usually the later the better.

> and their specs.

You mean like capabilities they supply and peripherals which will
be recognized?  This differs as well.  Mind you, for older hardware
the last time a corresponding SRM firmware was updated likely predates
existince of things like ATA specs - with obvious implications.

> Do I have to try ALL SRM's I can get a hold of and do the "trial-and-error"

You cannot.  Most of them will be unsuitable for your particular
hardware.  In practice you are limited to one or two variants for
your board - the newest you can dig out.  Sometimes you can find
various release notes which contain a real information.  It is also
good to ask, usually on the net, people who are already running
something similar as what you have.

> Isn't there any SRM-paradise somewhere out there that has done that already
> to spare me the effort?

Hm.  ftp.digital.com is usually a good first place to vist.  In
particular a directory /pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/ there.  Grepping
through index I found 98 files with "srm" as a part of their names.

Also Google is your friend. For example at
ftp://ftp.harddata.com/pub/kernel_Nautilus/srm/
you will find updated and fixed SRM for a Nautilus UP1500 board.
Things of that sort can be scattered also elsewhere.

> And if I shouldn't find a SRM that fits me, can you make your own ??
> "Do It Yourself SRM"    -How about that for a project...  =)

I am not sure if one can get enough of information about low level setup
of boards and processors to make that even possible to contemplate.  In
any case a value of work required to do that from scratch, if doable at
all, likely dwarfs a value of hardware where you would use results. The
other catch is you would have to produce executables in specific VMS
formats.  Not impossible but I do not know at this moment about any
tools other than VMS development kits.

  Michal


