That means that likely you do not have 'aboot.conf' although you are
trying to use it.  It behaves as designed.  BTW - why do you need that
self-made floppy?

> and I can not boot anymore.

????  Meaning...?

> what am I missing here.

That aboot.conf is an optional, convenience, feature and that from
'aboot> ' prompt you can type various boot commands explicitely.
You can also pass a location of a kernel to boot with '-file' option
and all its arguments using '-flags "......"' directly from an SRM
prompt.

Also on a 7.2 CD you can find an image of a boot floppy.  This is
ext2 file system which you can mount (you can do that over a loop
even without writing a floppy) on any Linux machine and peek what
is inside.  You will find a sample of 'aboot.conf' used on a floppy
there.

  Michal


